A||||9||GT Bastard||How hot do exhaust man/headers get?  ||||1||
Z||000000||GT Bastard||05-23-2000||07:29 PM||||Over 500 deg?  ||||reg||1
Z||000001||Sillhouette.com||05-23-2000||11:21 PM||||It depends if your timing is set properly or not.  I've seen manifold glow red in the dark when the timing was not set properly.  After fixing it the manifold never heated up that much and no damage was done.  <P>For headers so far all the headers I've dealt with were also powder coated.  So they are pretty cool.  You can keep your hand a short distance away from it and not get burned.  <P>Later||||reg||1
Z||000002||GT Bastard||05-24-2000||12:33 AM||||over 500 deg?||||reg||1
Z||000003||Cooter||05-24-2000||12:35 AM||||Yes, I was told the o2 sensor wont give a good reading until it gets over 500.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Standard||05-24-2000||03:46 AM||||the o2 sensor doesn't give reading's until it get's to 600 degrees F.||||reg||1
Z||000005||lowCG||05-24-2000||10:06 PM||||I'd say 1200,and powder coating is applied in a 400 degree oven,so that was probably something else,ceramic maybe?||||reg||1
Z||000006||Phaeton||05-24-2000||10:23 PM||||Exhaust gas temp works the O2 sensor.  The manifold runs a lot cooler.<BR>1200 degrees is starting into the orange glow, red usually starts  just past 900.  look at them when it is really dark or it doesn't count.||||reg||12
Z||000007||DRH||05-25-2000||11:34 AM||||If you are asking if 500 degree engine paint will stay on an exhaust manifold the answer is definitely no. The 1500 degree stuff might stay if it is cured properly in an oven.||||reg||1
Z||000008||mshill||05-25-2000||05:26 PM||||What DRH said!!!  My 1200 degree manifold paint was not baked in an oven and it is pretty much gone now (only 1000 miles).<P>||||reg||1
Z||000009||lowCG||05-25-2000||10:55 PM||||The trick is to get the paint hot enough to release some of the thinners,but not enough to blister,letting it cool each time after it gets hot enough to smell like paint,until it's cured.||||reg||1
